From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 17:28:21 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: isp: Add T6040 support

The T6040 (M4 Pro/Max) ISP is the T8140 coprocessor with the same H17
firmware interface: its register map in the macOS kext has the T8140
reset, control, fabric and interrupt words, and its firmware asks for
the 0x290-byte H16 boot descriptor. What differs is taken from how
macOS 26.6.2 boots it on the J616s (the firmware's own log of the boot
and of every command it receives) and from aurora's m1n1 proxy captures
on that machine:

 - The firmware is started as iBoot left it, with no reset sequence.
   The coprocessor must not have run yet and its reset vector must be
   the firmware text at the base of the heap region; the driver checks
   both instead of starting something else.
 - No clock enable word, legacy boot mode 1 in GPIO 6.
 - The boot descriptor carries the board's camera connection layout and
   a 0xde600000-byte shared window, as macOS passes it.
 - The broadcast-clear command takes four windows in eight slots, the
   unused ones all ones. Generalise the H17 command to several windows;
   T8140 still sends its single one.
 - Like T8140, the firmware reaches PMGR, PMP and DSID registers through
   its DART at their physical addresses, among them the clock and
   bandwidth scratch words named in PMP_CTRL_SET and the broadcast-clear
   bases. Map the J616s ADT's DART address filter ranges for the ISP
   1:1, rounded out to 16 KiB pages.
 - The firmware's main memory is 32 MiB at IOVA 0. iBoot loads __TEXT
   and __DATA into it, and they end 80 KiB short of the top (0x1fec000
   of 0x2000000). The rest is the host's to back, as macOS does; left
   unbacked, the firmware's first writes there take DART translation
   faults and it never answers its first command. Read the end of the
   "data" memory-region and map zeroed pages from there to the heap top
   for as long as the firmware surfaces live.
 - Output goes through scaler 1, so the rendered pool is the scaler 1
   pool, as the buffers submitted to it already were.
 - There is no capture metadata pool or capture watchdog. Skip that
   pool and its size check on H17 firmware that has none.

The front camera is an IMX958 (sensor 0x958, lens 0x29). Of the 32
setfiles in macOS's appleh16camerad, only module 2224 revision 01 names
it in section 0x50f, where each setfile names the sensor it tunes; 2924
and 2921 name the IMX614. With 2924 the firmware streams, but reports
a lens shading calibration for a 3744-pixel-wide sensor on every frame
("fullRes (3072 x 3024) lsCalibRes (3744 x 3024) not match!") and never
logs the module match macOS logs once the setfile has loaded. 2224
carries a 3072 x 3024 calibration. The file is 0x6ff4 bytes up to the
end of its section chain.

T6040 sets none of T8140's fw_iova_mask, mbox2_offset and
mbox_irq_route. The firmware's own addresses are at 0x100_0000_0000,
above the 36 bits T8140 keeps, so no mask is applied. Its doorbells have
a register window of their own in Apple's device tree, described as
"mbox2", instead of sitting inside the mailbox window. Only the base
interrupt enable is written; T8140's extra enable and routing words are
not needed on T6040.

Signed-off-by: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: LLM
(backported from commit 316136216030f8c857875f3e5751c7f4585f9468)
[j604: context conflict in apple_isp_probe() with official linux-aurora's J613/J615 temporal-filter quirk (4433ef5a0d, 7bdc7439d9, not in Aurora): kept both, quirk first.]
